# What this file is
#
# `Formula/keel.rb` no longer exists in this repository (#1023, #990). The tap —
# berkayturanci/homebrew-keel — pulled that exact path every thirty minutes, so
# until it was repointed at the release asset its sync job would have 404'd on a
# schedule and `brew upgrade` would have stopped seeing releases: the failure
# shape of #981, arriving from a new direction.
#
# The line above names the tap commit that applied
# packaging/homebrew/tap-sync-formula.patch — berkayturanci/homebrew-keel#3 — so
# the ordering (repoint, then merge, then release) is recorded rather than
# remembered. To reproduce it:
#
#     git -C ../homebrew-keel apply /path/to/keel/packaging/homebrew/tap-sync-formula.patch
#     git -C ../homebrew-keel commit -am "ci: sync the formula from keel's release asset"
#     git -C ../homebrew-keel push
#     echo "tap-sync-formula: $(git -C ../homebrew-keel rev-parse HEAD)" \
#       > packaging/homebrew/TAP_REPOINTED
#
# `tests/test_external_promises.py` requires that line offline, and — under
# KEEL_CHECK_EXTERNAL=1, which CI's `external promises` job sets — checks that
# the live tap really no longer names the retired path.
#
# Only the offline half blocks a merge. `external promises` is NOT among main's
# required status checks — those are the nine test-matrix jobs, Format (ruff),
# CodeQL, the pull-request description lint and the keel evidence check — so a
# red run there is visible and ignorable. Adding it to the required list is what
# would make the online half enforced, and that is an operator decision.
#
# The line above is therefore a claim in a reviewable form, not proof: nothing
# offline can read another repository, and the test accepts any 40 hex
# characters. Requiring a commit sha rather than a non-empty file is what makes
# the claim falsifiable — a reviewer can open the commit it names.
#
# Both the marker and its gate are single-use. Delete them once the tap has been
# repointed for a release or two and nobody is tempted to restore the committed
# formula.
